  "summary": "The Berkeley City Council voted 5-4 to extend its Flock Safety ALPR contract for up to 12 months, while postponing decisions on adding drones and fixed surveillance cameras and sending those to a competitive bidding process. The vote followed months of debate, community opposition citing immigration and civil liberties concerns, and Flock's assurances about fixes to past data-sharing problems.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The motion, from Councilman Brent Blackaby, extended Flock's ALPR contract for up to 12 months (from July); but sent the other technologies \u2014 drones, municipal and private surveillance cameras \u2014 to the city manager for a formal bidding process",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "Oakland and Richmond kept theirs. El Cerrito, Mountain View and Santa Cruz called theirs off.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "Now, it is not possible for a single California agency to share outside the state.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "At the end of the day, I just don't trust Flock",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    }
  ],
